    function debounce(func, wait) {
      let timeout;

      return function executedFunction() {
        const context = this;
        const args = arguments;

        clearTimeout(timeout);
        timeout = setTimeout(() => func.apply(context, args), wait);
      };
    }

  In this implementation, we create a debounce function that takes two arguments - func, which is the function we want to debounce, and wait, which is the number of milliseconds to wait before executing the function again. The returned function, executedFunction, is what will be called instead of the original function. It clears any existing timeout and sets a new one using setTimeout. The new timeout calls the original function after the specified wait time has passed. This way, the function will only be executed once every wait milliseconds, even if it is called multiple times within that period.

  Debouncing is different from throttling because debouncing ensures that the function is only executed once after a certain amount of time has passed, whereas throttling ensures that the function is executed at least once every certain amount of time, even if it is called multiple times within that period.

